  "lineage": "Congregational dhikr/zikr chanting practised across Sufi tariqas from the medieval Islamic world into modern Turkey, Egypt, Syria, the Maghreb and South Asia, codified in orders such as the Naqshbandiyya, Qadiriyya, Rifa'iyya and Mevleviyya — unison or responsorial repetition of divine names (Allah hu, la ilaha illa'llah) over a breath-locked pulse that accelerates from slow free declamation to a fast 2- or 4-beat panting ostinato, led by a solo munshid singing melismatic maqam phrases above the group, with frame drums (daf, bendir) and occasionally ney entering only in the faster stages.",
